Z||000000||BV MotorSports||07-05-2001||10:51 PM||||Hey all,<P>I have been having trouble with my computers DVD player lately. When playing any movie it will just "freeze", I have to hit ctrl-alt-delete and end task then re-start. Its a real pain in the butt! Soem moives seem to play longer between freeze-ups.<P>Its a Toshiba DVD-ROM SD-M1202. I also noticed that the DMA is unchecked but everytime I check it...it becomes unchecked. Supposedly this should be checked or the pitcure can "jump" or "skip" or even lock up.<BR>Please help, I know just enuff about 'puters to get myself in trouble.<P>Steven<BR>Stupid CyberMax (read= no customer support) <BR>AMD K62 450, 128MB RAM, 10g HD and some other crap too!||||reg||8
Z||000001||84Bill||07-06-2001||09:54 AM||||I have had this problem. I had replaced my video card and the freezing problems went away. I also replaced my mother board and processor a few weeks later with an AMD 1GHZ and an Azus MB I can now watch a DVD movie without the slightest problem.<P>You may not get a DMA assigned if the MB is an older one. The newer boards have UDMA 66 or 100 but most require the 80 pin cable. Look over your motherboards documentation. Check you BIOS settings for your CD rom there may be a setting for the DMA.<P>An AMD 1Ghz processor and MB will run you about 200 bucks now.<P>I almost forgot<BR>Check for updates for Windows and all your hardware like video cards and such. Some Updates will fix known problems like you described above<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||07-06-2001||09:59 AM||||Sounds like a HDD Controller problem.  If you can't turn on DMA (Or you turn it on and it forces off) that ususally means the controller doesn't support it, which is not suprising for a K6 series system.  Alot of machines from that vintage didn't support DMA.  With a machine of your power you NEED DMA support!  Otherwise it cannot transfer the data fast enough between the data transfer bus and the video card.<P>Time to upgrade.  I just finished an upgrade to a P4-1.7 Ghz, not my reccomendation, I would go AMD myself, but I went P4 at the time cause it was a deal.||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||07-06-2001||10:58 AM||||Nice system there Grinth, I believe your right on the nose about the controller, Some earlier motherboards have a DMA 66 but without the Driver for windows the DMA 66 will not work properly. <BR>BV I have that very same DVD CD Drive. Check you motherboard specs and see if it supports UDMA 66 or DMA 66 if it does then you may need to DL the drivers from your motherboard mfg's support site.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||BV MotorSports||07-06-2001||04:22 PM||||Ok you guys are starting to get way past my knowledge of 'puters.<P>Well My system is about 1yr old. Its just as it was when I bought it with the execption of more RAM (128 vrs 64). The DVD worked good up till about 4 months ago. Now it does what I said its doing.<P>Clueless in Va<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||11
